WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.627
Razor Pages applications commonly use
browser forms for account changes,

2
00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:15.060
profile updates, search filters,
approvals, edits, uploads, preferences,

3
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:18.194
and administrative workflows.

4
00:00:18.274 --> 00:00:21.668
Forms are productive and familiar,

5
00:00:21.748 --> 00:00:29.101
but a form that changes server-side state
needs more than a signed-in browser

6
00:00:29.181 --> 00:00:30.086
session.

7
00:00:30.166 --> 00:00:37.632
The application needs confidence that the
request came from the intended user

8
00:00:37.712 --> 00:00:39.070
interaction.

9
00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:46.276
A logged-in browser request does not
automatically prove request intent.

10
00:00:46.356 --> 00:00:51.334
Browsers carry cookies according to
browser rules,

11
00:00:51.414 --> 00:00:57.070
and those cookies may represent an
authenticated session.

12
00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:05.182
Antiforgery validation adds a
request-intent check for unsafe browser requests by

13
00:01:05.262 --> 00:01:13.294
requiring a value that the application
expects and validates before processing the

14
00:01:13.374 --> 00:01:14.731
state change.

15
00:01:14.811 --> 00:01:22.730
Framework form helpers and Razor
conventions often make antiforgery support feel

16
00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:23.715
routine.

17
00:01:23.795 --> 00:01:30.017
That is useful, but teams should still
review nonstandard forms,

18
00:01:30.097 --> 00:01:37.563
custom HTML, partial views, AJAX
submissions, JavaScript-driven requests,

19
00:01:37.643 --> 00:01:42.620
and handlers that sit outside the expected
pattern.

20
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:50.732
The places that differ from convention are
the places most likely to need attention.

21
00:01:50.812 --> 00:01:56.355
GET-style requests should not modify
server-side state.

22
00:01:56.435 --> 00:02:01.413
A request used for navigation, display,
filtering,

23
00:02:01.493 --> 00:02:08.620
or lookup should not approve an item,
change a setting, trigger a workflow,

24
00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.659
delete a record, or update a user's state.

25
00:02:12.739 --> 00:02:20.658
Keeping state changes in handlers that
expect unsafe methods makes request intent

26
00:02:20.738 --> 00:02:25.036
easier to reason about and easier to
protect.

27
00:02:25.116 --> 00:02:32.469
CSRF protection is separate from
authentication, authorization, and CORS.

28
00:02:32.549 --> 00:02:36.169
Authentication identifies the user.

29
00:02:36.249 --> 00:02:42.358
Authorization decides whether the user may
perform the action.

30
00:02:42.438 --> 00:02:48.207
CORS controls certain browser cross-origin
read behavior.

31
00:02:48.287 --> 00:02:53.717
Antiforgery validation addresses a
different concern:

32
00:02:53.797 --> 00:03:01.037
whether an unsafe browser request reflects
the expected user interaction.

33
00:03:01.117 --> 00:03:03.719
Failure handling matters.

34
00:03:03.799 --> 00:03:11.605
Token validation failures should be
predictable, safe, and useful to operators.

35
00:03:11.685 --> 00:03:19.377
The user-facing response should not expose
sensitive implementation details.

36
00:03:19.457 --> 00:03:27.149
The operational record should provide
enough context to understand which page,

37
00:03:27.229 --> 00:03:35.148
handler, and request category failed
without recording secrets or sensitive form

38
00:03:35.228 --> 00:03:36.020
values.
